    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Faults are common structures in rock impacting on subsurface fluid flow relevant to many economically significant problems including water supply, geothermal, petroleum, and mineral resources. One factor that influences the flow properties of faults is the occurrence of argillaceous rocks such as clay or shale within fault zones because these rocks have relatively low permeability. This study describes the geometry and structural features of shale in fault zones, and deciphers the mechanisms of their emplacement in a sedimentary rock sequence in the Sholomo Graben, southern Israel. It is hoped that this study will help to improve upon the present knowledge about:\r\n(1) When and how faults may incorporate shale into the fault zones therein acting as a lateral seal for hydrocarbons, and (2) when and how this sealing capability may breached.\r\n\r\nThis knowledge, in turn, will eventually help to develop a predictive technology for application to fault geometry and the assessment of its sealing potential at the subsurface.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
